    "problem": {
      "type": "content-columns",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "paper",
        "layout": "list",
        "numbered": false,
        "row_density": "compact",
        "eyebrow": "The problem",
        "heading": "Wet feet are a race problem, not a comfort problem.",
        "lede": "<p>On a long winter run the cost of soaked feet is not that they feel unpleasant. It is that wet skin blisters, and a blister at eight miles decides the rest of the run.</p>"
      },
      "blocks": {
        "p1": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Softened skin gives up early",
            "body": "<p>Wet skin under repeated rub is the blister mechanism. Dry skin takes considerably more of it before it breaks down.</p>"
          }
        },
        "p2": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Trail shoes are built to drain",
            "body": "<p>A mesh upper is designed to let water out, which necessarily means letting it in. That is the right design for the shoe. It still leaves the foot wet.</p>"
          }
        },
        "p3": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Cold arrives with the wet",
            "body": "<p>A soaked foot at winter temperatures gets cold quickly and stays cold, however hard the rest of you is working.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "p1",
        "p2",
        "p3"
      ]
    },
    "answers": {
      "type": "content-columns",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "ink",
        "layout": "list",
        "numbered": true,
        "eyebrow": "What answers it",
        "heading": "What it is, without the marketing.",
        "head_note": "<p>It is a three-layer sock with a membrane in it, so it is thicker than a racing sock. It is not a compression sock and it is not a lightweight. Knowing that is what lets you decide correctly.</p>",
        "link_label": "The three layers, in section",
        "link_url": "/#construction"
      },
      "blocks": {
        "a1": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "The membrane is the point",
            "body": "<p>A licensed Porelle\u00ae waterproof-breathable laminate between two knits. Water stops at the sock; vapour still moves outward.</p>"
          }
        },
        "a2": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Thicker than a race sock \u2014 that is the trade",
            "body": "<p>Three bonded layers are not as thin as a single-layer technical sock. On wet winter miles that trade is usually worth taking. In a dry summer 10k it is not.</p>"
          }
        },
        "a3": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Crew height, above the shoe",
            "body": "<p>The cuff clears a trail shoe collar, which is also where most of the spray and grit arrives.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "a1",
        "a2",
        "a3"
      ]
    },
