    "practice": {
      "type": "content-columns",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "paper",
        "layout": "list",
        "numbered": false,
        "eyebrow": "In practice",
        "heading": "Choosing honestly.",
        "link_label": "Size guide",
        "link_url": "/pages/size-guide",
        "link2_label": "Care & washing",
        "link2_url": "/pages/care-and-washing"
      },
      "blocks": {
        "w1": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Try them on a training run first",
            "body": "<p>Never on race day. Any change in sock thickness changes how a shoe fits, and a race is the wrong place to find that out.</p>"
          }
        },
        "w2": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Wet-weather kit, not everyday kit",
            "body": "<p>Plenty of runners keep a pair for winter and wet races and run in their usual socks the rest of the year. That is a reasonable way to use them.</p>"
          }
        },
        "w3": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Cool wash, no conditioner or dryer",
            "body": "<p>Standard care for anything with a membrane in it.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "w1",
        "w2",
        "w3"
      ]
    },
    "limits": {
      "type": "centre-note",
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "blue",
        "max_width": 46,
        "eyebrow": "What it will not do",
        "heading": "The honest limit.",
        "body": "<p>Breathable does not mean dry inside. Run hard enough and you will sweat faster than any membrane can move vapour \u2014 so on a hard effort your feet will be damp from the inside, at body temperature, rather than cold from a puddle. That is a real difference. It is also not the same as dry.</p>",
        "footnote": "<p>Every limit we know about is published, not only this one. <a href=\"/#limits\">Read the rest</a>.</p>"
      }
    },
